[0.003, "o", "\r\u001b[2K\u001b[1mThe machine rejected the dividend because it exceeds 10 digits. We need to compute the remainder \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1musing a different approach. The problem statement says: \"for a bigger number, n d mod gives the \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mremainder of any 20-digit n directly\". This suggests we can use a word `n d mod` that takes a \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m20-digit `n` and a divisor `d` and gives the remainder. But `n d mod` is not listed in the word \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mlist. Maybe we can use the `mod` word with a different strategy, or we can break the number into \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mparts. Alternatively, we can use the fact that the machine might have a way to handle larger numbers\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mvia `*/` or other tricks. Let's read the machine reference again: \"arithmetic  + - * / mod ( a b -- \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mc )   /mod ( a b -- rem quot )   */ ( a b c -- a*b/c )   negate abs 1+ 1- 2* 2/ ( a -- b ) ... / and\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m/mod: the dividend may have up to 10 digits (for a bigger number, n d mod gives the remainder of any\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m20-digit n directly).\" It seems `n d mod` is "]
[0.000, "o", "not a defined word, but maybe we can define it? Or \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mmaybe we can use the fact that the machine's `mod` word actually works for any numbers up to 20 \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mdigits? The error says \"/ dividend over 10 digits\". Maybe `mod` also has the same limit? Let's test \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m`mod` with the same numbers: `82225043186179281 6469193 mod .` but `mod` is not listed as a word \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mthat takes two numbers? Actually the word list has `mod` in arithmetic: `+ - * / mod ( a b -- c )`. \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mSo `mod` is a word. Let's try `mod`. But the error was for `/`. Maybe `mod` works? Let's try a block\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mwith `mod`. If it also fails, we need another method. We can also use the property of modular \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1marithmetic: we can break the large number into smaller chunks and use `mod` repeatedly. For example,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mwe can express the number in base 10 and use `mod` on chunks. But we have a Forth machine; we can \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mwrite a program that does division by repeated subtraction or using the machine's `*/` and `mod` i"]
[0.000, "o", "f \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1mwe can somehow make it work. Alternatively, we can use the fact that the machine might have a word \u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m`n d mod` that we can call if we know its address? Not likely.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
